# Windows alternate-data-stream artifacts, created when files are copied into
# WSL from Windows. Pure noise — never commit them.
*:Zone.Identifier

# LaTeX build products. The .bbl is the exception and IS tracked: arXiv does not
# run BibTeX, so the .bbl is a submission artifact, not a build artifact.
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.blg
*.toc
*.lot
*.lof
*.synctex.gz
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.bcf
*.run.xml
*.nav
*.snm
*.vrb

# Compiled output. Rebuildable from source; keep the repo to sources so diffs
# stay readable. Remove this line if you would rather archive each submitted PDF.
main.pdf
contamination-disclosure.pdf

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
*~
.vscode/

# Working documents, deliberately untracked: kept in .private/ and not part of
# the repository. The rule is simple — this repository tracks only what is
# useful to a reviewer, which is the released audit instrument and the README
# that points at it. Everything that is an operational note to ourselves lives
# in .private/.
#
# PENDING-STEPS.md   our own to-do list; names dates, judgement calls and the
#                    private mirror. Nothing a reviewer needs.
# MIRROR-MANIFEST.md how the anonymisation is done — which, as its own text
#                    says, is itself a signal. It also contained a local home
#                    directory path, i.e. a username, in a tracked file.
REVIEW-RESPONSE.md
NEXT-STEPS.md
OSF-Instructions.md
PENDING-STEPS.md
MIRROR-MANIFEST.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Manuscript sources, deliberately UNTRACKED for the duration of the
# double-blind review. The submitted version lives outside version control
# at .private/Paper_Contamination_Disclosure/. Re-track at camera-ready.
#
# NOTE: untracking removes these from the index going forward. It does NOT
# remove them from the existing history — earlier commits still contain the
# full manuscript and the author block.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
main.tex
references.bib
main.bbl
contamination-disclosure.bbl
README-arxiv.txt

# Anonymity check patterns. UNTRACKED ON PURPOSE: a tracked file listing the
# strings that identify you is itself the leak it is meant to prevent. Copy
# .anon-patterns.example to .anon-patterns and fill it in locally.
.anon-patterns

# Retired assets and working copies, kept outside the repo.
.private/

# Saved copies of the audit/state-of-play report. Working documents, not part
# of the submission or the released artifact.
Contamination Disclosure Audit.pdf
*Audit*.pdf
__pycache__/

# Archive snapshots taken by hand. `.private.zip` contains the whole private
# directory and `.git.zip` the whole history including the author block — both
# would be catastrophic to commit, and neither is reviewer-useful.
*.zip

# Per-coder packs, built by audit/make-coder-kit.py after the freeze. Derived
# entirely from tracked files, and operational rather than reviewer-facing —
# each folder is addressed to one named person and is sent to them directly.
coder-kit/
# ...except the per-coder worklists and briefings: audit-check.py reads the
# worklists, and the briefing is what the checklist says was released.
!coder-kit/R1/
!coder-kit/R2/
!coder-kit/*/worklist-*.md
!coder-kit/*/START-HERE.md
worklist-*.md

__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/

# Author-facing working notes; 4open.science serves README.md as the landing page.
MAINTAINER.md
